A||||12||btoth||Signs of air in the lines?||||5||
Z||000000||btoth||12-08-2001||04:22 PM||||What are symptoms of air in brake lines?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mach10||12-08-2001||04:50 PM||||Squishiness. Spongy-feel... Pedal sinks slowly after you hammer it down...||||reg||12
Z||000002||West Coast Fiero||12-08-2001||05:07 PM||||sometimes ( but not always ) a bubbling sound in the master cylinder when brakes are depressed<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-09-2001||11:04 AM||||Air in lines will leave a mushy pedal that feels springy under load... but once it's pumped up it usually holds pressure.<P>If it sinks slowly, it's usually a sign a seal is failing.<P>Low, but solid, pedal could be the MC or a rear caliper problem.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||btoth||12-09-2001||12:59 PM||||Well, maybe this should start another thread, but...<P>I had the braided steel lines installed in my car.  (Along with pads and new parking brake lever.)  I got the new lines to try and improve the "spongy" feel that I had since I bought the car.  The only way I could stop fast was to literally stand on the brakes.  And the more stoping I did, the more effort it would take to stop.  (Like rush-hour traffic.)<P>Well, with the new lines, I new it would get all new fluid too, so I thought that would be an added plus.<P>Well, now, the car does stop good with little effort (don't know if it's the new pads or the lines), but the pedal has no resistance <I>at all</I>.  I can push it down with no effort and the brakes don't really do anything till the pedal is all the way down it seems.  But once I manage to reach that far they stop great!  And the pedal does seem to actually rest higher than it was before I had the stuff done to it (which should be a good sign).<P>So, I was just trying to figure out if they screwed up something.  Should I just drive it around a bit and see if anything improves?  Or should I re-bleed the brakes?<p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 12-09-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000005||btoth||12-09-2001||10:50 PM||||"Movin' on up...." *bump*||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-09-2001||11:39 PM||||The "normal" height of the pedal under breaking load in most cars, including Fiero, is at or slightly above the resting height of the gas pedal.<P>If the pedal is dropping below the gas pedal when stopping then one of the following is happening...<P>MC is bad<BR>Rear caliper out of adjustment or bad.<BR>air in line.<BR>one or more wheel bearings sloppy/bad. (this causes the rotor to move laterally which pushed the piston into the caliper bore beyond spec.)<P>Having to "stand" on the pedal to stop may also mean the power booster has a problem. It could be a dead booster, a bad/dirty check valve, or bad/blocked vac line. In car with the filter in the booster line, a blockage is a very real posibility.<P>You assumed the fluid would be replaced.... dolt! most shops only install enough new fluid to replace whatever is lost to bleeding. This is NOT the same as a flush of the system.<P>A total flush is a giant pain in the as_ but keep in mind.... new fluid and old fluid will make usually NO differance in peddal feel. The main issue in new vs. old is boiling temp and frankly most systems don't heat even the crappiest fluid enough to boil in normal driving. If they did, the accident rate would be far higher than it is. The majority of cars on the road exceeded FMVSS min wet boil long ago.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||4
Z||000007||88-DOHC||12-10-2001||01:59 AM||||The only thing that I might add that others havn't mention is that it is VERY important you bleed the brakes in the order of furthest away from the MC.  So, first one to bleed is right rear, second left rear, third right front, and last left front.  If that wasn't the case, you could still have air in the line.||||reg||1
Z||000008||btoth||12-10-2001||09:48 AM||||It wasn't my usual machanic that did the work this time, it was some new guy.  He also didn't put 3 of my lug nut covers back on  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> so the owner of the place said he'd have to order some for me, and the plastic trim at the bottom of the door around the parking brake lever is broken and missing a screw.  So I wouldn't be surprised if something got screwed up.<P>The brakes stop a lot easier than before I had the work done, but the pedal has no resistance at all.  No "feel" to it.  And the distance it needs to be pushed is farther than before.<P>I'll mention it to him when I go to pick up my lugnut covers.  And I guess I have to make a trip to the junk yard for a replacement for the broken plastic.||||reg||1
Z||000009||btoth||12-10-2001||11:01 AM||||Also, the pedal doesn't return to it's resting spot very quick.  I can't remember if that's normal or not.<P>(I was spoiled with BMW brakes for a week while my car was in the shop.)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||Alex2004||12-10-2001||11:39 AM||||I had that problem before with the brakes having no resistance.  Probably air in the lines.  But with mine my right rear caliper broke a seal inside of it and it didn't come in the rebuild kit so i just got another caliper.  That could be the problem.  Bleed brakes..and if it happens again, check for leaks in the calipers. Or just replace the rear calipers anyway because they probably need it.<P>Alex...||||reg||1
Z||000011||btoth||12-11-2001||11:18 AM||||Another thing...<P>Before I had the brake work done.  When the car was off and I pumped the brakes, the pedal eventually went stiff and I couldn't move it.<P>Now, with the car off, the pedal will get stiffer, but I can still push it down.  Is this how it's supposed to be?||||reg||5
Z||000012||88-DOHC||12-11-2001||04:30 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B>Another thing...<P>Before I had the brake work done.  When the car was off and I pumped the brakes, the pedal eventually went stiff and I couldn't move it.<P>Now, with the car off, the pedal will get stiffer, but I can still push it down.  Is this how it's supposed to be?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Nope, that alone pretty much says you have air somewhere in the system. A properly bleed brake system should behave like you described above (before you had the work done).  <BR>||||reg||1
